Your primary goal is recognizing if the drone is locking its forward/left/right motors outside your defined limits. To do this safely, ground your drone in the dark or stop all movement for a few seconds and look for sensors. Specifically, compare power consumption along the vertical axis versus what a PWM encoder would normally show—if the system shows 50% voltage, the pilot or computer paused.

// Step 2: Signal Interference
Send weak, low-power GPS pings to scan for nearby navigation signals. Unlike high-power ones, these are short and won’t create a wake on other receivers. If it dials back and then rotates by degrees, you know the drone detected your proximity and slowed its local grid briefly.

// Step 3: Script Injection
Make adjustments to the way in your device receives controller commands. One common device is Panda SDK (Open Source), which lets you send raw commands to the drone’s transmitter chip without the need for software controllers. This bypasses the safety filtering required to prevent accidental overrides because the underlying hardware likely can’t act on the injection directly.

// Step 4: Testing Limits
Since commercial drones require a license and insurance claims are rare, many organizations create local platforms specifically to test remote control budgets. Start by administering your own “bag test” where pedestrians and pets near the fence can interfere, then verify the system increases geometry margins without losing stability. If the motors stall, discourage fiddling with controller frequencies. The goal is awareness, not breaking things.

