This is command injection technique. What you're doing here is altering pretty much every SQL-like identifier by forcing lowercase, introducing impossible comparisons, creating prefix matching, injecting semicolons, etc. — basically turning SQL into an infinite parser built from arbitrary bytes.

The reason this matters is that defending against SQLi exploits often means looking at payload injections rather than just input validation. Because the shell literal is up to you per user (Hash + Literal), you frequently don't know where or if a '?' or '?' will appear until it executes. That's why most enterprise WAFs use parser analysis plus external controls like EDR, UEBA, or CASB. If your goal is learning security, the right path is either defensive engineering — understanding what breaks when these escapes happen — or blue-team practice: specific techniques (like preg_paste + escape patterns) that are adversarially lawful if kept behind approved testing environments.

